I have no idea what's common lexicon in Britain, but baton and truncheon
have always been generally synonymous where I am.

'Baton' from a 1982 Macquarie (that's the Australian English dictionary):
a stick or club, esp. as used by someone in an official position.

In modern English, the terms aren't at all restrictive.  'Baton' can
refer to a modern telescoping baton, a side-handled baton, or even older
Victorian era club-like truncheons.  'Truncheon' can technically refer to
any of these as well, though such usage is indeed uncommon (probably
because use of the word 'truncheon' at all is fairly uncommon these
days).

